expiration-absolute-value: no signed overflow for expiration = INT32_MIN

Classification: spec-deviation bug fix.

Specification (TPM 2.0 Part 3, "Enhanced Authorization (EA) Commands",
"Signed Authorization Actions", "Policy Parameter Checks"): "*expiration* -
If this parameter is not zero, then: [...] if *nonceTPM* is not an Empty
Buffer, then the absolute value of *expiration* is converted to milliseconds
and added to *policySession→startTime* to create the *timeout* value [...] If
*nonceTPM* is an Empty Buffer, then the absolute value of *expiration* is
converted to milliseconds and used as the *timeout* value". Part 3,
TPM2_PolicySecret/TPM2_PolicySigned Command tables: "INT32 | expiration |
time when authorization will expire, measured in seconds [...]".

ComputeAuthTimeout() computes the absolute value as `expiration =
-expiration` on the INT32, which overflows (undefined behavior; in practice
it stays INT32_MIN) for expiration = -2147483648, and the sign-extended value
then gives a timeout of (2^64 - 2^31) * 1000 instead of 2147483648 * 1000
milliseconds; the output timeout and the policy ticket's HMAC (which covers
the timeout) differ from tpm-rs, which uses the absolute value 2^31. The fix
takes the absolute value in UINT64 arithmetic. Found by differential fuzzing
(TPM2_PolicySecret with expiration 0x80000000).
diff --git a/src/Policy_spt.c b/src/Policy_spt.c
--- a/src/Policy_spt.c
+++ b/src/Policy_spt.c
@@ -190,18 +190,19 @@
 	policyTime = 0;
     else
 	{
-	    if(expiration < 0)
-		expiration = -expiration;
+	    // tpm-rs differential testing -- C-ref spec deviation fix (NOT determinism):
+	    // the absolute value of expiration without the signed overflow of
+	    // -expiration for INT32_MIN (Part 3, "Policy Parameter Checks").
 	    if(nonce->t.size == 0)
 		// The input time is absolute Time (not Clock), but it is expressed
 		// in seconds. To make sure that we don't time out too early, take the
 		// current value of milliseconds in g_time and add that to the input
 		// seconds value.
-		policyTime = (((UINT64)expiration) * 1000) + g_time % 1000;
+		policyTime = ((expiration < 0 ? (UINT64)0 - (UINT64)expiration : (UINT64)expiration) * 1000) + g_time % 1000;
 	    else
 		// The policy timeout is the absolute value of the expiration in seconds
 		// added to the start time of the policy.
-		policyTime = session->startTime + (((UINT64)expiration) * 1000);
+		policyTime = session->startTime + ((expiration < 0 ? (UINT64)0 - (UINT64)expiration : (UINT64)expiration) * 1000);
 	}
     return policyTime;
 }
